A||N||9||Monkeyman||Idle problem||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||07-01-2001||05:45 PM||||My car ('86 GT) idles poorly when it's cold.  You really have to play with the gas (at idle) to keep it from dying.  Once your moving, it gets better but only above about 2k.  Below that you take the chance of killing it.  I've tried a couple of tanks of Berrymans B12.  It seemed to help slightly, but definitely hasn't cured the problem.  Any suggestions before I take it down to the shop?  New tuneup (plugs, wires, cap, rotor, etc)?  New injectors?  Something worse?<P>TIA<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||5
Z||000001||beachbomb||07-01-2001||07:58 PM||||Hi, i've replaced all those on my 4 cyc and still have the rough idle when engine is cold, i just live with it for that 2-3 minutes. if you find out exactly what can fix it, plmk, thanks..jim(2 '84 Indys) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigzach_7.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000002||87GTZ34||07-01-2001||10:11 PM||||What is the fuel pressure like?  How long since you've changed the fuel filter?  How many miles on the injectors?  Do you see my direction on this?  It sounds like a fuel problem IMO.  You may have a problem when it is warm (lack of power) but it is not noticable (unless on the dyno) but when cold the problem is significant enough that it is below the threshold of a smooth running engine with little load.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||07-02-2001||03:40 AM||||You got me a little lost (not hard to do).  The fuel filter was just changed last week and the injectors have almost 200k on them.  I have absolutely no idea what the fuel pressure's like.  If I need new injectors, I'll probably just learn to live with it for a couple of years until I'm ready for a new motor.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||07-02-2001||04:50 AM||||changing your engine temp sensor?<P>does it idle normally when its warmed up and not die out?||||reg||1
